{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01M7bSsYbhd81x7w84dYXXjV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context carefully to understand the current state and determine what tasks to propose.\n\n## Project Summary\n\nThis is a Go reimplementation of GNU coreutils/moreutils utilities, verified via differential testing. The project uses a specification-driven approach where SRDs define requirements, and an AI code generation pipeline (cobbler) generates Go implementations.\n\n## Current State Analysis\n\nLooking at the `requirement_states` in the project context, I can see which SRDs have requirements in \"ready\" state:\n\n1. **srd004-ts** - R3.3, R3.4, R4.1, R4.2, R4.3, R5.1, R5.2, R5.3, R6.1, R6.2, R7.1, R7.2, R7.3, R8.1, R8.2, R9.1, R9.2, R10.1, R10.2, R10.3\n\n\n2. **srd105-stty** - R7.2, R7.3\n3. **srd110-pr** - R2.1, R2.2, R2.3, R3.1, R4.1, R4.2, R4.3, R5.1, R5.2\n4. **srd111-ptx** - R2.1, R2.2, R2.3, R3.1, R4.1, R4.2, R5.1, R5.2\n\nNow I'm examining the codebase to see which utilities already have implementations and what work remains.\n\nLooking at the release 15.0 items, all four components—ts, stty, pr, and ptx—show as not started. But I need to check the earlier releases more carefully since the constraint is to focus on the earliest incomplete work. In release 00.0, I can see some utilities are already implemented like magefiles, sys, hashutil, sizeparse, and encutil, while format and testutils haven't been started yet.\n\nScanning through the subsequent releases, most are fully implemented, but there are scattered incomplete items: cat in 01.1, nproc in 03.1, ls in 04.0, logname in 04.1, echo and tac in 05.0, expand in 05.1, cut in 05.2, sha1sum and sha512sum in 05.4, mktemp in 06.0, and groups in 06.1. I'm realizing there's a critical constraint I need to apply: I can only propose tasks for requirements that have \"ready\" status in the requirement_states. Looking at what's available, that's just four items from release 15.0 — ts, stty, pr, and ptx. Everything else either isn't listed or doesn't have the right status, so those are off the table regardless of their implementation progress. I can see the generation run has already tackled tasks for ts requirements up through R3.2, so the next ready requirements start at R3.3. I need to understand what those requirements actually entail, but the SRD content isn't fully included in the project context, and I'm constrained from using tools to look them up. This is a blocker since I can't propose meaningful tasks without knowing what the requirements specify. Looking at the ready requirements for srd004-ts, I can see there are twenty spread across groups R3 through R10. The recent commits mention R1.5, R1.6, and several R2-level requirements, which suggests there's earlier work I should account for when calculating the total weight.\n\nThe roadmap description indicates that weight-4 requirements get their own groups, but I don't have access to the actual weights from requirements.yaml. I'll need to work with the assumption that most requirements have a default weight of 1, unless the group structure itself signals otherwise. Since I'm constrained from reading files directly, I should propose tasks conservatively and note which requirements I'm including so the weights can be verified against the actual configuration. be cautious.\n\nLooking at the groupings:\n- R3: R3.3, R3.4 (2 sub-reqs)\n- R4: R4.1, R4.2, R4.3 (3 sub-reqs)\n- R5: R5.1, R5.2, R5.3 (3 sub-reqs)\n- R6: R6.1, R6.2 (2 sub-reqs)\n- R7: R7.1, R7.2, R7.3 (3 sub-reqs)\n- R8: R8.1, R8.2 (2 sub-reqs)\n- R9: R9.1, R9.2 (2 sub-reqs)\n- R10: R10.1, R10.2, R10.3 (3 sub-reqs)\n\nThat's 20 sub-requirements just for ts, but I'm limited to 4 tasks maximum with each task holding at most 4 sub-requirements. Since I need to cover ts, stty, pr, and ptx across those 4 tasks, I'll need to focus on the next sequential batch. Based on the recent commit pattern where tasks bundle exactly 4 sub-requirements, the next task should include R3.3, R3.4, R4.1, and R4.2, though I should verify the weights first. I don't see any single-sub-requirement groups in the ready set, so I'll assume each requirement has a weight of 1 and batch them into groups of 4. I'm planning out four tasks for the TypeScript implementation: the first two tasks cover R3.3 through R5.3, and the third task handles R6.1 through R7.2, each staying within the weight limit of 4.\n\nThe fourth task would cover R7.3 through R9.1, leaving R9.2 and R10.1-R10.3 for TypeScript along with all the work for stty, pr, and ptx in future tasks. Given that all four SRDs are in the same release and TypeScript already has recent commits, I should prioritize completing the TypeScript work first before moving to the other use cases. I'm inferring the requirement groups based on the commit patterns I've seen—they seem to progress through basic timestamping, format modes, relative time conversion, incremental and elapsed time features, custom formatting, error handling, signal management, and differential testing. Since I can't access the actual SRD content, I'll work with these educated guesses to write meaningful task descriptions.\n\nLooking at the existing task structure, these are implementation tasks for the cmd/ts tool that modify the main implementation and test files. I need to reference the SRD file in required_reading, map each task to its specific requirement ranges, and define clear acceptance criteria based on what those requirements likely cover.\n\nSince I'm batching requirements with a maximum weight of 4 per task and assuming unit weight for each sub-requirement, I can fit about 4 sub-requirements per task. The dependencies flow sequentially since they're all modifying the same files, so each task builds on the previous one.\n\nGiven the constraint of 4 tasks maximum and the large number of ready requirements across all SRDs, I need to decide whether to focus all 4 tasks on the ts implementation or spread coverage across multiple SRDs. Focusing on ts would give me 16 sub-requirements total, but spreading across SRDs might provide better overall progress.\n\nSince ts already has momentum with recent commits and is the first incomplete work in the 15.0 release, I should continue there. The pattern from recent commits shows how to structure the task names and requirements. Now I'm working out the specific task breakdown, starting with the first task that has no dependencies, then chaining the remaining three sequentially. I need to figure out what each requirement group actually covers for the ts utility. Looking at the features—timestamp formats, custom strftime strings, relative and incremental timestamps, monotonic clock support, UTC versus local time—these probably map to the SRD's R1 through R10 groupings. Since I'm seeing weight-4 tasks split into separate groups with 2-3 sub-requirements each, I should just reference the SRD directly and let the stitch agent pull the actual requirement text rather than guessing at the specifics.\n\nNow I'm ready to write the task descriptions following the output format, making sure to include the SRD as required reading along with the existing implementation files and test utilities so the agent has everything needed to understand the context. So none of these groups have multiple sub-requirements, which confirms they're all weight-1. Now I'm mapping out the full set of ready requirements for TypeScript—eight requirement groups totaling twenty sub-requirements that I need to include in my output.\n\nWith a four-task limit of four sub-requirements each, I can cover sixteen of them, leaving R9.2 and R10.1 through R10.3 for future work. Let me reorganize the grouping to keep related requirements together: pairing the remainder of R3 with the start of R4, completing R4 and all of R5 together, then R6 with the beginning of R7.\n\nFinishing R7 while completing R8 and starting R9 rounds out the fourth task. This leaves the remaining requirements for the next batch.\n\nSince these tasks need to be independently executable, the stitch agent will need to read the current state of the main file to extend it properly. Now I'll construct the final output following the required format carefully.\n\nI realize the key is referencing the SRD in the required_reading section so the agent can look up the full specification details, then writing requirement text that points to those sections rather than duplicating all the details inline. Now I'm ready to write the final output, making sure to follow the commit message convention with the requirement IDs properly formatted.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":43571,"cache_read_input_tokens":11845,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":43571},"output_tokens":25,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"25f178c2-bf1a-4e14-a0f4-804457f21770","uuid":"4692155f-9d91-4e99-b1d0-0db76928ca4f"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01M7bSsYbhd81x7w84dYXXjV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: go-unix-utils reimplements GNU coreutils/moreutils in Go, verified by differential testing against Homebrew reference binaries. The goal is to stress-test a spec-driven AI code generation pipeline.\n\n**Architecture**: SRDs define requirements per utility. The cobbler orchestrator (measure + stitch) generates Go code in `cmd/` and `pkg/`. `pkg/testutils` provides the differential testing harness, `pkg/sys` wraps syscalls, `pkg/format` handles output formatting. Each `cmd/<utility>/` contains `main.go` and `<utility>_test.go`.\n\n**Current state**: Generation run 44 is active on branch `generation-run44`. Recent commits completed srd004-ts R1.5 through R3.2 (tasks 5646, 5647). Many utilities across releases 00.0–14.0 are partially or fully implemented. Release 15.0 (ts, stty, pr, ptx) is in progress.\n\n**Current release**: The only requirements with `ready` status are in release 15.0:\n- srd004-ts: R3.3–R10.3 (20 sub-requirements)\n- srd105-stty: R7.2, R7.3 (2 sub-requirements)\n- srd110-pr: R2.1–R5.2 (9 sub-requirements)\n- srd111-ptx: R2.1–R5.2 (8 sub-requirements)\n\n## Priority Reasoning\n\nAll ready requirements are in release 15.0. Within 15.0, ts (srd004-ts) is the first use case and already has active work (R1–R3.2 completed). Continuing ts is the correct priority. With 20 ready sub-requirements and a max of 4 tasks at 4 sub-reqs each, I'll cover R3.3 through R9.1 (16 sub-reqs), leaving R9.2 and R10.1–R10.3 for the next batch.\n\n## Weight Budget Checks\n\nAll ready ts requirements are in multi-sub-requirement groups (R3 has 2 remaining, R4 has 3, R5 has 3, R6 has 2, R7 has 3, R8 has 2, R9 has 2, R10 has 3). Per the roadmap description, \"each weight-4 requirement is in its own group\" — since all these groups have multiple sub-requirements, they are weight-1 each.\n\n- Task 0: R3.3(w=1) + R3.4(w=1) + R4.1(w=1) + R4.2(w=1) = 4 ≤ 4 ✓\n- Task 1: R4.3(w=1) + R5.1(w=1) + R5.2(w=1) + R5.3(w=1) = 4 ≤ 4 ✓\n- Task 2: R6.1(w=1) + R6.2(w=1) + R7.1(w=1) + R7.2(w=1) = 4 ≤ 4 ✓\n- Task 3: R7.3(w=1) + R8.1(w=1) + R8.2(w=1) + R9.1(w=1) = 4 ≤ 4 ✓\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"[stitch] cmd/ts implementation (srd004-ts R3.3, R3.4, R4.1, R4.2)\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/software-requirements/srd004-ts.yaml (SRD with full requirement definitions for R3.3, R3.4, R4.1, R4.2)\n      - cmd/ts/main.go (existing ts implementation to extend -- R1.5 through R3.2 already implemented)\n      - cmd/ts/ts_test.go (existing differential tests to extend)\n      - pkg/testutils/difftest.go (DiffTest struct and RunDiffTests for differential testing pattern)\n      - pkg/sys/sigpipe.go (InstallSIGPIPEHandler -- already called in main)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/ts/main.go\n        action: modify\n        note: Add functionality for srd004-ts R3.3, R3.4, R4.1, R4.2\n      - path: cmd/ts/ts_test.go\n        action: modify\n        note: Add differential test cases covering R3.3, R3.4, R4.1, R4.2\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: Implement srd004-ts R3.3 as specified in the SRD\n      - id: R2\n        text: Implement srd004-ts R3.4 as specified in the SRD\n      - id: R3\n        text: Implement srd004-ts R4.1 as specified in the SRD\n      - id: R4\n        text: Implement srd004-ts R4.2 as specified in the SRD\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: Follow the existing code structure in cmd/ts/main.go -- extend the current flag parsing and timestamp logic rather than restructuring\n      - id: D2\n        text: Use the differential testing pattern from pkg/testutils with RunDiffTests comparing against the reference binary (ts from Homebrew moreutils)\n      - id: D3\n        text: Call exec.LookPath(\"ts\") for the reference binary and t.Skip if not found, per project conventions\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: cmd/ts/main.go compiles without errors (go build ./cmd/ts/)\n      - id: AC2\n        text: go vet ./cmd/ts/ reports no issues\n      - id: AC3\n        text: All new differential test cases pass when the reference binary is available (go test ./cmd/ts/ -count=1)\n      - id: AC4\n        text: Existing tests for R1.5 through R3.2 continue to pass (no regressions)\n\n- index: 1\n  title: \"[stitch] cmd/ts implementation (srd004-ts R4.3, R5.1, R5.2, R5.3)\"\n  dependency: 0\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/software-requirements/srd004-ts.yaml (SRD with full requirement definitions for R4.3, R5.1, R5.2, R5.3)\n      - cmd/ts/main.go (existing ts implementation to extend -- R1.5 through R4.2 already implemented)\n      - cmd/ts/ts_test.go (existing differential tests to extend)\n      - pkg/testutils/difftest.go (DiffTest struct and RunDiffTests for differential testing pattern)\n      - pkg/sys/sigpipe.go (InstallSIGPIPEHandler -- already called in main)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/ts/main.go\n        action: modify\n        note: Add functionality for srd004-ts R4.3, R5.1, R5.2, R5.3\n      - path: cmd/ts/ts_test.go\n        action: modify\n        note: Add differential test cases covering R4.3, R5.1, R5.2, R5.3\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: Implement srd004-ts R4.3 as specified in the SRD\n      - id: R2\n        text: Implement srd004-ts R5.1 as specified in the SRD\n      - id: R3\n        text: Implement srd004-ts R5.2 as specified in the SRD\n      - id: R4\n        text: Implement srd004-ts R5.3 as specified in the SRD\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: Follow the existing code structure in cmd/ts/main.go -- extend the current flag parsing and timestamp logic rather than restructuring\n      - id: D2\n        text: Use the differential testing pa